  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Canadian high pressure will bring dry conditions\nwith low humidity to start the work week.\n\nIn the wake of a cold frontal passage this morning, a rather \nstrong dome of Canadian high pressure works its way eastward \nfrom the Great Lakes. Over the next couple of days, this frontal\nzone is to settle along the southeastern U.S. coast while a \n1028-mb ridge tracks through New England. This will favor \nabundant sunshine, dry weather, cooler temperatures, as well as \nmuch less humidity. In fact, dew points temperatures are likely \nto drop into the mid/upper 40s. Daily highs through Tuesday \nshould be in the mid 70s to low 80s (60s to low 70s across the \nmountains). The dry nature of the air mass will support cool \novernight periods, particularly tonight. With the ridge axis \nright over the northeastern U.S., forecast lows tonight will \nfall into the upper 40s to mid 50s. The I-95 urban corridor \ncould stay slightly milder being in the upper 50s. \n\nAmong the days ahead, today should prove to be the breeziest\ngiven the tight gradient formed by the departing front and Great\nLakes high pressure. Northwesterly gusts up to 20 to 25 mph are\nexpected this afternoon, possibly to near 30 mph in a few spots.\nExpect winds to drop off after dark with the decrease in\nboundary layer mixing. Winds turn lighter by Tuesday with high\npressure in place. By mid-week, this ridge eventually begins to\nshift off the Atlantic coast. This will support a gradual warm\nup as winds return to southerly. Wednesday's forecast highs push\nback into the mid/upper 80s, with mainly 70s for the mountains.\nDespite an uptick in humidity, the pattern does remain dry.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm and dry conditions expected through\nThursday; showers possible along a cold front at the end of the\nweek.\n\nA strong upper ridge builds over the region during the middle \nportion of the week supporting very warm and dry conditions. Not \nlooking at any record highs since records are in the mid 90s. The \nridge begins to flatten during the second half of the week as it \nstarts feeling pressure from a deepening trough over Hudson Bay. A \nfront is expected to progress southward at the end of the week and \nbring chances of showers and thunderstorms. While we should start \nseeing a cooling trend next weekend, temperatures still look to be \nabove normal. However, there has been noticeable model run-to-run \nchanges and large spread seen in temperatures for next weekend which \ndecreases confidence in the temperature forecast.",
